# Appendix F — The Cart Format & Where Games Live

Chapter 16 shipped a game by calling `save-cart` and got a file back. This
appendix opens that file: what a cart *is* on disk, where the console keeps it,
the one rule its name must follow, and how you open, edit, and finish a game
across sessions. Everything here is something the console will confirm at the
prompt — so it does.

## A cart is a snapshot you can round-trip

The whole point of a cart is that what you save is exactly what you get back.
Light a pixel, save, wipe the screen, load, and read the pixel again:

```motoi
(pset 3 4 12)
(save-cart \"mygame\")
(pset 3 4 0)
(display (pget 3 4))
(newline)
(load-cart \"mygame\")
(display (pget 3 4))
(newline)
```

```
0
12
```

Read the two lines. After `save-cart` you wiped `(3, 4)` back to `0`, and `pget`
confirmed the wipe — `0`. Then `load-cart` pulled the saved snapshot back and
`(3, 4)` is `12` again. The cart carried the *real* pixel, not a description of
one. `save-cart` writes the console's state to disk; `load-cart` restores it;
what comes back is byte-for-byte what went in.

## What the file actually holds

The cart is a plain-text file you can open in any editor. It opens with a short
readable header, then one line of JSON holding the whole machine:

```
; motoi scheme cart snapshot
; created 2026-07-26T20:13:29.375Z
; version 1
#!json-blob
{\"version\":1,\"created\":\"…\",\"framebuffer\":{\"w\":80,\"h\":80,\"color\":14,
 \"frame\":0,\"palette\":[[0,0,0,255],…16 colors…],\"pixels\":[…6400 ints…]},
 \"audio\":{\"timeline\":[],\"bpm\":120}}
```

Nothing here is opaque. The header names the format and stamps the time. The
`framebuffer` is the screen from Appendix C — `80` by `80`, its sixteen-color
`palette`, and all `6400` `pixels` as color numbers. The `audio` block is the
transport from Appendix E — a `timeline` of events and the `bpm`. A cart is the
screen and the sound chip, frozen and written down. Because it is JSON after a
labelled header, a person *or* another program can read it.

## The name is a bare filename — nothing else

A cart name is a *filename*, not a path. The console refuses anything that could
climb out of its folder — no slashes, no `..`, no leading dots. Catch the refusal
and see it happen:

```motoi
(display
  (guard (e (#t 'rejected))
    (save-cart \"../evil\")))
(newline)
```

```
rejected
```

`save-cart` saw `\"../evil\"` try to escape the carts folder and raised an error,
which the `guard` caught and turned into `rejected`. This is the console's
sandbox: a cart can only ever land *inside* the carts directory, so loading one
someone handed you can never overwrite a file somewhere else on your disk. Give
carts plain names — `mygame`, `level-2`, `boss-fight` — and they behave.

## Where games live on disk

Save one cart and remember one fact, then look at what the console made:

```
~/motoi/
├── carts/
│   └── mygame          ← every save-cart lands here, by bare name
└── cortex.slatl        ← what the console remembers (Chapter 15)
```

`~/motoi/` is the console's home for *your* things. `carts/` holds every game you
save — one file per cart, named exactly what you passed to `save-cart`.
`cortex.slatl` is the memory from Chapter 15, the facts that outlive a session.
The console creates these the first time it needs them; you never set them up.

## Opening, editing, and finishing a game

There is no separate 'project' to manage — a cart *is* the game. The whole
lifecycle is three verbs and the language you already know:

- **Open it.** `(load-cart \"mygame\")` restores the screen and sound. Now the
  console holds that game's state; drawing and playing continue from where the
  cart left off.
- **Edit it.** Run any code — `pset`, `fb/rect`, `world/spawn`, `synth/play`.
  You are changing the live console, exactly as in every chapter. The cart on
  disk does not change until you save.
- **Finish it.** `(save-cart \"mygame\")` writes the current state back under the
  same name, replacing the old snapshot. Save under a *new* name to keep both —
  `(save-cart \"mygame-v2\")` — and you have branched your game.

That is the entire model: load to open, run code to edit, save to keep. A game is
never 'half-installed' or 'locked'; it is one readable file you overwrite when
you are happy with it.

> **Tip**
>
> Save often, and save under new names at milestones — `level-1`, `level-1-boss`,
> `level-1-final`. Because a cart is a plain file, keeping five of them costs you
> five small files and buys you five points you can always load back to. There is
> no undo like a fresh `load-cart` of the last good save.

> **Warning**
>
> There is no separate 'drafts' or 'broken-games' folder — every `save-cart`
> writes straight into `carts/`, and saving under a name that already exists
> **replaces** it. If you want to keep the old version, save the new one under a
> new name first. The console will not warn you before overwriting; the bare-name
> you pass is the file you land on.

